in a nutshell a very bad night for the  two big parties and a very good one for  the Lib Dems but if your choice conclude  this was all about an anti or pro brexit  vote have a look beneath the surface and  you'll see it was a bit more complicated  than that so while the Tories lost  councillors in most places they gained  them in northeast Lincolnshire an area  that voted strongly for brexit taking  control there for the first time ever  and now look at Chelmsford which also  voted leave the Tory vote cratered and  the Lib Dems now have control labour won  Trafford a big metropolitan districts  they haven't held it in 17 years but  they also lost control of bowls over the  territory of veteran MP Denis Skinner 14  seats lost 11 of them to independence  it's the weakest Labor's ever been in  that council as for the Lib Dems the  news was almost entirely positive  winning Tory councils not just in Essex  and East Anglia but across the southwest  places like Bath and North East Somerset  that's Jacob Riis Mark's own backyard so  what would happen if you projected all  of these results to the House of Commons  well according to us to follow just  Michael Thrasher the Tories would end up  with a very similar share of the vote  but convert it into parliamentary seats  and this is what you get 280 seats for  the Tories 268 for labour 28 for the Lib  Dems 53 for the SNP and 21 MPs from  other parties and it would imply guess  what another hung parliament but the  Tories will be so short of an overall  majority the most likely coalition party  would be labour plus the SNP plus maybe  the Lib Dems and I'm not sure that's a  projection that will fill either of the  two big parties with much in the way of  enthusiasm
 

